{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat decisions/D022* decisions/D023* decisions/D014* decisions/D019* decisions/D011* decisions/D015*'","aggregated_output":"# D022 — D020 completed + matched static layer offload vs engine LRU expert cache (x86-main, c2-resume)\nClaim category: runtime-placement/offload trade-off between two EXISTING llama.cpp 31288c9c6 features. Not an engine-code gain.\nConditions: image llamacpp-b0 (sha256:cf383f82...), mixE4K (sha256 2e520c4a...; hashed by orchestrator op, lab spec now names mixE4K), chat prompt, -n 160, -c 4096, temp 0, seed 1, --no-warmup, -ngl 99; one GPU job; host-load monitor: no compiler active (artifacts/x2/*hostload.log; first monitor self-matched its own pattern, verified + fixed; Codex O's objects all predate the D020 window).\n1. D020 rerun: job-8df3c7bb27 att-2 completed 10/10 under the T-M runner (2cf2925); st_prose_n18 flagged invalid_utf8 (the original crash site) and still scored (completion parser policy).\n2. Matched job job-ad883810b3 (lab@2cf2925, 9 configs x 3 rounds, order alternated per round). Medians of decode t/s [3 samples]:\n   static n24 742 MiB 128 | n18 902 MiB 152 | n12 1064 MiB 197 | n6 1226 MiB 323 | n0 1388 MiB 684\n   moe-cache c128 870 MiB 54 | c256 998 MiB 99 | c384 1126 MiB 187 | c640 1382 MiB 458\n   At every matched footprint static layer offload is faster (≈2.8x at ~880 MiB, ≈2x at ~1030, ≈1.7x at ~1180, 1.5x at ~1385). Output text identical (stdout sha256 equal) across all configs.\n3. Interpretation (consistent with D019, not proven by it): LRU-with-admission pays H2D on every miss; layer offload pays none. Neither is expert-granular AND admission-free -> T-O static hot-expert candidate is the untested quadrant.\nLimits: one prompt, decode only (prefill not compared), single host, discrete-only (PCIe). 3 samples/config.\n# D023 — NO_VMM engine + budget shim: what is counted, what is enforced (x86-main)\nClaim category: infrastructure/methodology validation. Evidence: evidence/claude-code.jsonl task X3; artifacts/x3-shim/probe/{c4096-b0,c4096-novmm1,cap900-ngl99,cap900-auto}.{jsonl,out}.\nImage: campaign/llamacpp:novmm1 local id sha256:ec351fcd... (OCI index digest under the containerd store), llama.cpp 31288c9c6, label campaign.cmake.defines=-DGGML_CUDA_NO_VMM=ON. Build compiled (307 s) but Build CC v2 FAILED at inspect_image (T-N regression: required containerimage.config.digest which containerd-store Buildx never emits) -> record status=failed; image identified manually here; fix delegated as T-N2. Not a qualified release until T-N2 rebuild passes.\n1. -c 4096, mixE4K, -ngl 99, no cap: shim sees weights 778.7 + KV 192 + compute 108 MiB; NO_VMM adds 3 pool allocations (~0.1 MiB) that the VMM build hid. Shim peak 1078.9 MiB vs ~1388 MiB nvidia-smi delta for the same config (D022) -> ~310 MiB context/cuBLAS/driver overhead uncounted.\n2. GPU_BUDGET_MIB=900 + forced -ngl 99: KV allocation denied, engine aborts cleanly (rc=1, \"cudaMalloc failed: out of memory\"), denial logged. Enforcement of intercepted paths works and failure is observable.\n3. GPU_BUDGET_MIB=900, no -ngl (llama.cpp auto-fit reads virtual meminfo): fit placed only 155 MiB on GPU; 86 t/s decode — worse than static n24 (128 t/s @742 MiB real). Auto-fit is very conservative under the virtual budget -> budget experiments must set placement explicitly (n-cpu-moe / hot set) within the cap; do not use auto-fit as the baseline.\nBudget statement for future runs: cap applies to intercepted allocations only; report shim peak AND nvidia-smi delta; declare n_ctx.\n# D014 — Expert-granular slot cache: PREDICTION from real routing traces + measured static-offload calibration\nEvidence: artifacts/traces/out/{prose,code,chat}.jsonl (Granite BF16, llama-routing-trace built via Build CC patch path, rt1: 18 s ccache build), experiments/routing/{prose,code,chat}.{json,md} (trace_replay), experiments/routing/predict_slots.py -> prediction.json; calibration job-36b7630119 (static --n-cpu-moe sweep, engine b0, mixE4K).\nObserved (real): routing is skewed but only moderately: with 16 of 32 experts resident per layer, decode hit rate 70% (prose), 78.5% (code), 72% (chat) vs 50% for uniform; 24 slots: 88-94%. Code > prose > chat at 16 slots => domain-dependent (advice: do not generalise).\nMeasured static layer offload (discrete GPU, PCIe-dependent => DISCRETE-ONLY): mixE4K GPU MiB / decode t/s: n0 1218/704, n6 1056/336, n12 894/212, n18 734/161, n24 576/128. Memory falls ~27 MiB per layer moved; speed falls steeply because a layer's whole expert set runs on the CPU.\nCalibration (assumption: linear): 33.3 us per CPU-computed expert activation; 0.836 MiB per resident expert slot (mixE4K).\nPREDICTED (not measured): at ~897 MiB, 16 slots/layer => 298-358 t/s serial (vs static 212), up to 517-704 if CPU misses overlap GPU hits; at ~736 MiB, 8 slots => 182-214 serial (vs static 161). Cold-start hit rates (conservative; warm caches higher).\nLimits: logical trace, cold-start per phase; assumes miss cost linear and no PCIe transfers; Granite is not a stress model; the 1B/32-expert statistics must not be extrapolated to the target model (needs target traces).\nValidation needed: a real hit/miss dispatch implementation (Strata-style) => delegated to focused harness (cc/hybrid_expert). Until then this is a hypothesis with a stated discriminating experiment.\n# D019 — hybrid_moe_bench (Opus T-J) result on the D014 prediction (all timings UNSCORED/exploratory; discrete GPU; synthetic activations; replayed routing)\nEvidence: cc/hybrid_expert/{RESULTS.md,results.json,results_L12_single_layer.json}, artifacts/opus-hybrid/, evidence/opus-hybrid.jsonl (ops op-eb0f9a7be446 build 350 s; op-2ea9dea3e2e5 24-layer run 62 s).\n- Correctness: all-hit hybrid == GPU reference and zero-hit hybrid == CPU-all, bitwise, all 24 layers; mixed hit/miss within 1.5x the larger endpoint error vs fp64 (CPU quantizes activations Q8_K, CUDA Q8_1 => backends differ by float noise by construction).\n- Miss cost: 30.6 us marginal per CPU-computed expert + ~15 us fixed per layer with any miss (DRAM-bound ~29 GB/s) -> confirms the 33.3 us calibration (D014). AVX2 Q4_K dot confirmed by disassembly.\n- Serial model within -11%/+6% on 24 layers: K16 measured 2808 us/step vs predicted 2996. SUPPORTED (single-layer-in-isolation timing misleading: L3-resident).\n- Perfect-overlap bound NOT reached (0.06-0.33 of hideable time saved; 25-35 us/layer of CUDA launch work shares the CPU thread): D014's 517-704 t/s upper range is NOT supported by this implementation. Serial form stands: ~330 t/s at ~897 MiB derived (vs 212 measured static n12) — derived, not measured end to end.\n- H2D admission 37.9 us/expert pinned, 90 us pageable ~= CPU compute cost => LRU that admits every miss loses (admission traffic 1853 us/step at K16 > CPU time saved; hit rate 74.4% vs static 73.3%). Static hot set ~ LRU hit rate with zero admission traffic and CUDA-graph friendliness.\n- Existing engine feature found: llama.cpp 31288c9c6 has --moe-cache-mib (uploads misses). Test it end to end (next).\nStatus of H-slotcache: serial prediction supported at single-layer-harness level; end-to-end still unmeasured.\n# D011 — Bandwidth screen (ncu) and Q8_0 decode profile; engine candidate selected\nEvidence: artifacts/profiles/tg_matmul_ncu.{ncu-rep,csv} (ncu 2025.1.1, SYS_ADMIN, profiling-only timings, NOT inference speed); q8_tg128_{node,graph}.{nsys-rep,sqlite}.\nBF16 (D008): MoE expert matvec (grid 512x8, 16.8 MB, 25 us) = 75% DRAM peak (~665 GB/s, spec peak 896 GB/s is a published value, not measured here); attention projections (1-2 MB, 4-6 us) only 30-44% => latency-bound; mul_mat_f 63%. => BF16 kernel-level headroom small (<~7% overall); weight bytes are the lever (confirmed by Q8_0 +32% decode, D009).\nQ8_0 decode: graph exec median 1171 us + gap 178 us => 741 tok/s (profiled graph-level; unprofiled screen 794). 633 kernels/token (vs 487 BF16): +146 quantize_q8_1/token = 14.4% of kernel time, avg 1.6 us each.\nKernel order in a layer shows 3 separate quantize_q8_1 of the same normed activation before the q, k, v matvecs (artifacts/profiles/q8_tg128_node.sqlite).\nSelected engine candidate C1 (H-fuse-q8): reuse one q8_1 quantization of an activation across consecutive consumers. Prediction: remove ~2 quantize launches/layer (~48/token, ~53 us of 1171) => ~3-4.5% decode. Success criterion (frozen before assessment): >= +2% decode on matched counterbalanced A/B (A/A bound 0.5-2%), outputs bit-identical (mean KLD 0, top-1 100% vs unpatched Q8_0), no PPL change. If <2% or non-identical => rejected, reported as such.\nNot chosen now: BF16 kernel tuning (<=7% ceiling), graph-opt env (rejected D008), fusing norm+quantize (larger change, defer).\n# D015 — SSD-tier emulation via container memory limit (job-6e6b2d39b8; engine b0, mixE4K, --n-cpu-moe 24, own-file cold cache)\nClass: MIXED. Transfers: the existence/shape of the page-cache cliff, I/O accounting method, cold-vs-warm discipline. Does NOT transfer: absolute t/s (discrete GPU, Zen2 CPU AVX2, PCIe, local NVMe, Linux page cache; target is unified memory with its own SSD).\nMethod: all expert weights file-backed in host memory (GPU holds attention+embeddings only: 572-576 MiB), `docker --memory L --memory-swap L`, posix_fadvise(DONTNEED) on the model file only (no host-wide flush), cgroup memory.peak and io.stat rbytes sampled at 4 Hz.\nResult (pp512 / tg64, 3 reps; CONCURRENT COMPILE LOAD avg ~18 on 16 threads => speeds are lower bounds): limit -> tg t/s, SSD read: 1536m -> 129, 1.6 GiB; 1024m -> 79, 4.1 GiB; 768m -> 15, 28.8 GiB; 640m -> 3, 95.7 GiB; 560m -> 1, 135 GiB. All runs exit 0, no OOM kill.\nInterpretation: working set ~ process anon (~400 MiB incl. CUDA/ggml buffers) + ~750 MiB expert bytes. Once the limit drops below it, decode becomes SSD-bound with heavy read amplification (reads >> useful bytes: 135 GiB for a run of a few hundred tokens). Advisor bound tokens/s <= effective SSD B/s / fetched B/token is consistent with the ~15 t/s point if nearly every expert is re-fetched each token (~160 MiB/token if all 192 activations miss).\nImplication for the target: naive mmap + OS LRU has no notion of routing skew or layer order; an explicit hot-expert residency policy + prefetch/read-ahead control is the component to evaluate (trace-driven sizing in D014 gives the hit rate that policy needs).\nCaveat to fix in a clean rerun: CPU contention; 1 repetition per limit; the 1536m row includes the initial model load reads.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
